@page "/script"

<CatHead Title="The script">
    <code>sednaUi</code> holds generic UI behaviour only: theme settings, clipboard, toasts,
    confirmations, and delegated wiring for menus and tabs. Every class on these pages applies
    with scripting blocked — the script adds behaviour, never appearance.
    <a href="/hover-hints">Hover hints</a> have a page of their own.
</CatHead>

<div class="callout callout-info">
    <i class="ri-code-s-slash-line"></i>
    <div class="callout-body">
        <span class="callout-title">From C#, inject <code>ISednaUi</code></span>
        Register it once with
        <code>builder.Services.AddSednaUi()</code>. Every member is a JavaScript call, so
        <strong>none of them can run during prerendering</strong> — call them from an event handler, or
        from <code>OnAfterRenderAsync(firstRender: true)</code>. The wrappers do not swallow that
        exception.
    </div>
</div>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Interop.ToastsFromCsharp" Title="Toasts from C#"
            Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>ToastAsync</code> takes the message and a <code>ToastKind</code>, whose members are the
        CSS modifier suffixes, so <code>ToastKind.Go</code> and <code>.toast-go</code> cannot drift
        apart. There is no C# equivalent of the remover function <code>toast()</code> returns —
        dismissing a toast early stays a JavaScript concern.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Interop.SettingsFromCsharp" Title="Appearance settings from C#"
            Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>ISednaSettings</code> is the applied settings as state, with a <code>Changed</code>
        event — where <code>ISednaUi.LoadSettingsAsync</code> only answers what they are now, which
        goes stale when a reader on “System” changes their OS between light and dark. Call
        <code>StartAsync</code> from <code>OnAfterRenderAsync(firstRender)</code>, and check
        <code>IsLive</code> before drawing a toggle, or someone who chose light sees “dark” selected
        for one frame.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Interop.ConfirmFromCsharp" Title="Confirmation from C#"
            Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>await Ui.ConfirmAsync(…)</code> returns a <code>bool</code>, so the decision reads as
        ordinary C# control flow. Built on <code>&lt;dialog&gt;.showModal()</code> rather than
        <code>window.confirm()</code>, which in Blazor Server blocks the circuit while it is open.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatSnippet Name="Script/Loading.html" Title="Loading it">
    <p>
        Three files, and the order matters. <code>boot.js</code> goes in
        <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> so the stored theme is applied before first paint; at the end of
        the body it would produce a dark flash on a light-theme app.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>

<CatSnippet Name="Script/TimeZoneCookie.html" Title="The browser's time zone">
    <p>
        <code>data-tz-cookie</code> names a cookie, and <code>boot.js</code> writes
        <code>Intl.DateTimeFormat().resolvedOptions().timeZone</code> to it before first paint, with
        <code>path=/</code>, <code>SameSite=Lax</code> and a year's <code>max-age</code> — only when
        the value differs from the cookie already there. No reload, no event, no C# surface.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>A first request carries no cookie yet.</strong> Configure a fallback zone for that
        one render and let it stand — do <em>not</em> reload to get the cookie, because the next
        navigation already carries it.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Script.Toasts" Title="Toasts" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>sednaUi.toast(message, opts)</code> creates the stack on first use and returns a
        function that removes that toast early. <code>timeout: 0</code> keeps it until dismissed, and
        the message is inserted as <em>text</em>, never as markup.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Script.Confirmation" Title="Confirmation" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>await sednaUi.confirm({ … })</code> resolves <code>true</code> or <code>false</code>.
        With <code>danger: true</code> the confirm button turns red <em>and</em> focus starts on
        <strong>Cancel</strong>, so a stray <kbd>Enter</kbd> does not delete anything. Use it instead
        of <code>window.confirm()</code>, which blocks the Blazor Server circuit while it is open.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Script.CopyWithoutHandler" Title="Copy without a handler" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>data-copy="…"</code> copies a literal; <code>data-copy-target="#sel"</code> copies
        that element's text; an empty <code>data-copy-target</code> inside a
        <a href="/structure"><code>.code-block</code></a> copies its <code>&lt;pre&gt;</code>. The
        click is delegated from <code>document</code>, so a button rendered by a later Blazor render
        works with nothing wired.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Script.DelegatedMenu" Title="Menus, wired for you" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>data-menu-toggle</code> on the trigger inside a <code>.menu-anchor</code> handles
        opening, one panel at a time, the outside click, <kbd>Escape</kbd>, and returning focus to
        the trigger. The closed state is the <code>hidden</code> attribute, so a closed panel is out
        of the tab order; for a full-width anchor set <code>align-self: stretch</code> on it.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Script.DelegatedTabs" Title="Tabs, wired for you" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>data-tabs</code> on a <code>.tabs</code> container is mostly there for the keyboard:
        arrow keys, <kbd>Home</kbd> and <kbd>End</kbd> move between tabs, only the selected tab is a
        tab stop, and the panel named by <code>aria-controls</code> is shown while the rest are
        hidden. Leave <code>data-tabs</code> off when the component drives
        <code>aria-selected</code> from C#, or two things set it.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatSnippet Name="Script/SelectRefresh.txt" Title="Repairing a customizable select">
    <p>
        A <code>.form-select</code> that writes its own
        <code>&lt;button&gt;&lt;selectedcontent&gt;</code> shows a browser-made copy of the chosen
        option in its closed box, and <strong>Blazor's render does not trigger that copy</strong> —
        so without this the box is blank, with nothing in the console. The script repairs it for you;
        <code>sednaUi.select.refresh()</code> is here for the case the observer cannot see.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>
